+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a duration into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`.
+A duration is either a whole number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more
+`<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once and in that
+order, without spaces, like `2d`, `1h30m`, or `1d2h3m4s`. Units are
+case-insensitive.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait)
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
